# `module::Chord::Chord`

[Back to `module::Chord`](../Chord.md)

Builds a Chord empty, or from a dictionary of assets in one expression.

## Signature

```cpp
module::Chord::Chord();
module::Chord::Chord(std::initializer_list<module::Chord::Entry> assets);
explicit module::Chord::Chord(pluss::RangeView<module::Chord::Entry> assets);
```

## What It Does

The default constructor makes an empty Chord, to be filled with [`add`](add.md). The other two take the assets at construction, so a book of many assets needs no chain of `add` calls. `module::Chord::Entry` is `std::pair<std::string, module::Field>`: a name and the Field it names. The initializer-list constructor is the dictionary form, `Chord book{{"spx", spx}, {"ndx", ndx}}`, written like a map literal; the `RangeView` constructor takes any contiguous list of entries (a `std::vector` built in a loop, a `std::array`, a span), which is read once and not kept.

The assets are added in the order given, and that order is meaningful: it is the order of every result, of [`names`](names.md) and of the asset axis of the [correlation matrices](../Chord.md). That is why the dictionary is an ordered list and not a `std::map` or `std::unordered_map`, which would reorder the assets by name or arbitrarily and silently attach the wrong correlation row to an asset. The list is checked as a whole before anything is built: an empty name, a name repeated within the list, throws and no Chord is made.

A Chord built from model parameters rather than from Fields is made by [`from_model_params`](from_model_params.md).

## Parameters

`std::initializer_list<module::Chord::Entry> assets`
: The assets as `{name, field}` pairs, in the order they will be added. Names are non-empty, unique and case sensitive. Each Field is copied.

`pluss::RangeView<module::Chord::Entry> assets`
: The same from a contiguous list of entries. The constructor is `explicit`, so a Chord is not made from a vector by accident.

## Returns

A Chord holding the given assets, with `parallel` true and the correlation matrices undefined.

## Errors

Throws `subsp::math::finance::module::Chord::add: the asset name must not be empty`, `... there is already an asset named '<name>'`, or `... the asset name '<name>' appears twice in the list`. The constructors use the checks of [`add`](add.md).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: an empty Chord

The default constructor makes an empty Chord with its defaults.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  mod::Chord chord;
  out.writeln("size", chord.size(), " parallel", chord.parallel, " correlations defined", chord.price_corr.defined());
}
```

### Example 2: a dictionary of assets

The assets in braces, named and ordered like a map literal, priced in one call.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static mod::Field bs(double spot, double strike, double vol, double maturity = 1.0) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, strike, maturity), .market = fin::market::make(spot, 0.02, 0.01),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})}, .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  mod::Chord book{{"spx", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.20)}, {"ndx", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.30)}, {"rut", bs(50.0, 55.0, 0.35, 0.5)}};
  const auto result = book.price();
  out.writeln("assets", book.names(), " engine calls", book.batches());
  for (const auto &name : book.names()) out.writeln(name, result[name].price.items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: a list built in a loop

Many assets from data: fill a vector of entries and pass it to the RangeView constructor.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static mod::Field bs(double spot, double strike, double vol, double maturity = 1.0) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, strike, maturity), .market = fin::market::make(spot, 0.02, 0.01),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})}, .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  std::vector<mod::Chord::Entry> entries;
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) entries.emplace_back("strike" + std::to_string(90 + 5 * i), bs(100.0, 90.0 + 5.0 * i, 0.25));
  const mod::Chord ladder(entries);
  const auto result = ladder.price();
  out.writeln(ladder.names());
  for (const auto &name : ladder.names()) out.write(result[name].price.items<double>()[0], " ");
  out.writeln();
}
```

### Example 4: the same book either way

The dictionary, the vector and a chain of add build the same Chord.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static mod::Field bs(double spot, double strike, double vol, double maturity = 1.0) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, strike, maturity), .market = fin::market::make(spot, 0.02, 0.01),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})}, .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  mod::Chord chained;
  chained.add("a", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)).add("b", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.3));
  mod::Chord braced{{"a", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)}, {"b", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.3)}};
  std::vector<mod::Chord::Entry> list = {{"a", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)}, {"b", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.3)}};
  const mod::Chord from_list(list);
  out.writeln(chained.price().price.items<double>());
  out.writeln(braced.price().price.items<double>());
  out.writeln(from_list.price().price.items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: a single asset in braces

One entry in braces is still a dictionary of one.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static mod::Field bs(double spot, double strike, double vol, double maturity = 1.0) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, strike, maturity), .market = fin::market::make(spot, 0.02, 0.01),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})}, .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  mod::Chord one{{"only", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)}};
  out.writeln(one.size(), one.names());
}
```

### Example 6: the errors

A repeated name and an empty name refuse the whole list, and no Chord is made.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static mod::Field bs(double spot, double strike, double vol, double maturity = 1.0) {
  return mod::Field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, strike, maturity), .market = fin::market::make(spot, 0.02, 0.01),
                    .model = fin::model::BlackScholes{.volatility = pluss::make_series({vol})}, .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { mod::Chord c{{"a", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)}, {"a", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.3)}}; });
  report([&] { mod::Chord c{{"a", bs(100.0, 100.0, 0.2)}, {"", bs(90.0, 95.0, 0.3)}}; });
}
```


